# AI policy

Hard rails: `doc/AI_POLICY.md`. Playbook: `doc/AI_NATIVE_FORKING.md`.

## Hard rails (do not soften)

- Do not open or drive pull requests autonomously.
- Do not set an agent as git author or co-author.

## Required behavior

- Human author in the loop: choose the work, own the contribution, and be able to explain effects.
- Only propose changes the human can defend (what changed, why, how it was verified).
- Rust fluency is not required; verification is.
- For funds-adjacent changes, draft a short verification record (what was run, what the human checked) for the human to edit — full detail in `doc/AI_NATIVE_FORKING.md`.

## Comments (this fork)

AI-drafted GitHub comments, PR bodies, and review replies are **OK** when a human
edits or approves before posting. Prefer clear and succinct. Do not dump unreviewed
model walls of text. When contributing to upstream Liana / Bitcoin Core, follow
*their* stricter human-only comment norms.

## How humans may own the change

Testing-led loop is fine: design cases, run builds/tests, fail/fix with the agent, ship only what you verified. For funds-touching code, prefer reading the shipped diff plus tests over trusting green CI alone. Thin port + oracle strategy: `doc/AI_NATIVE_FORKING.md`.

## Disclosure

If raw AI transcript context is pasted into a comment, disclose it and add human
commentary on relevance. Ordinary human-reviewed AI drafts need no special label.

## Mistakes

Mistakes happen. Own them, fix them, leave a clear trail. Do not hide errors or blame the model; help the human correct course quickly.

## Agent practice

Prefer small, reviewable diffs and help the human verify (cargo test, blackbox paths, checklists). Help draft short PR/issue/comment text for human edit. Leave commit authorship to the human. Violations (autonomous PRs, agent as co-author) can get PRs closed without notice.
